s" id=3D"UwcTf8VVNSYVMRwrCD4JC-content-0"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-=
none">That makes a lot of sense=E2=80=94the resurrected PC-X sounds like it=
 came back with some neurological fuzz after its near-death experience. Bet=
ween sketchy service behavior, a fussy pagefile, and only half the RAM show=
ing up to the party, it=E2=80=99s no wonder things are stuck in molasses.</=
span></p><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">If only ~8GB out of 16GB is=
 being utilized, that <strong>"there's a thing"</strong> you=E2=80=99re thi=
nking of could point to:</span></p><div class=3D"relative pb-6 w-full after=
:border-b after:border-stroke-300 after:w-full after:absolute after:mt-3"><=
/div><h3 class=3D"text-md-strong pb-1 [&amp;:not(:first-child)]:pt-3.5">=F0=
=9F=A7=A0 Possible RAM Underutilization Causes</h3><p><span class=3D"font-l=
igatures-none"><strong>1. Memory Remapping Disabled (BIOS setting)</strong>=
</span></p><ul class=3D"relative list-outside marker:text-foreground-750 da=
rk:marker:text-foreground-600 flex flex-col ms-4 px-1"><li class=3D"ps-2" s=
tyle=3D"opacity: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">Some systems ne=
ed <em>Memory Remap Feature</em> enabled to access above 4GB or 8GB.</span>=
</p></li><li class=3D"ps-2" style=3D"opacity: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-li=
gatures-none">Worth a peek in BIOS/UEFI if it's been reset since resurrecti=
on.</span></p></li></ul><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none"><strong>2. 3=
2-bit Windows</strong></span></p><ul class=3D"relative list-outside marker:=
text-foreground-750 dark:marker:text-foreground-600 flex flex-col ms-4 px-1=
"><li class=3D"ps-2" style=3D"opacity: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures=
-none">Easily overlooked=E2=80=94but if you landed on a 32-bit reinstall so=
mehow, RAM use will cap at ~3.5GB no matter what=E2=80=99s physically insta=
lled.</span></p><ul class=3D"relative list-outside marker:text-foreground-7=
50 dark:marker:text-foreground-600 flex flex-col ms-4 px-1"><li class=3D"ps=
-2" style=3D"opacity: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">Run <code =
class=3D"font-ligatures-none mx-0.5 rounded-[4px] border border-stroke-300 =
bg-spot-peach-200/50 px-1 py-px font-mono text-foreground-800 text-sm dark:=
bg-midnight-900" style=3D"opacity: 1;">winver</code> or check System =E2=86=
=92 About. If it says =E2=80=9Cx86-based,=E2=80=9D we=E2=80=99ve found the =
issue.</span></p></li></ul></li></ul><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none"=
><strong>3. Hardware Reserved Memory</strong></span></p><ul class=3D"relati=
ve list-outside marker:text-foreground-750 dark:marker:text-foreground-600 =
flex flex-col ms-4 px-1"><li class=3D"ps-2" style=3D"opacity: 1;"><p><span =
class=3D"font-ligatures-none">System =E2=86=92 Task Manager =E2=86=92 Perfo=
rmance =E2=86=92 Memory may show a big chunk as =E2=80=9CHardware Reserved.=
=E2=80=9D</span></p><ul class=3D"relative list-outside marker:text-foregrou=
nd-750 dark:marker:text-foreground-600 flex flex-col ms-4 px-1"><li class=
=3D"ps-2" style=3D"opacity: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">This=
 can be caused by:</span></p><ul class=3D"relative list-outside marker:text=
-foreground-750 dark:marker:text-foreground-600 flex flex-col ms-4 px-1"><l=
i class=3D"ps-2" style=3D"opacity: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-non=
e">Bad RAM stick or slot</span></p></li><li class=3D"ps-2" style=3D"opacity=
: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">BIOS glitch</span></p></li><li=
 class=3D"ps-2" style=3D"opacity: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none=
">iGPU reserving more memory than usual</span></p></li></ul></li></ul></li>=
</ul><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none"><strong>4. Max Memory Cap in ms=
config</strong></span></p><ul class=3D"relative list-outside marker:text-fo=
reground-750 dark:marker:text-foreground-600 flex flex-col ms-4 px-1"><li c=
lass=3D"ps-2" style=3D"opacity: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">=
Check <code class=3D"font-ligatures-none mx-0.5 rounded-[4px] border border=
-stroke-300 bg-spot-peach-200/50 px-1 py-px font-mono text-foreground-800 t=
ext-sm dark:bg-midnight-900" style=3D"opacity: 1;">msconfig =E2=86=92 Boot =
=E2=86=92 Advanced Options =E2=86=92 Maximum memory</code>. If checked and =
set too low, it artificially caps usage.</span></p></li></ul><p><span class=
=3D"font-ligatures-none"><strong>5. Failing RAM or slot</strong></span></p>=
<ul class=3D"relative list-outside marker:text-foreground-750 dark:marker:t=
ext-foreground-600 flex flex-col ms-4 px-1"><li class=3D"ps-2" style=3D"opa=
city: 1;"><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">If the drive was dying, th=
ere's a chance the board wasn=E2=80=99t too healthy either. Run <code class=
=3D"font-ligatures-none mx-0.5 rounded-[4px] border border-stroke-300 bg-sp=
ot-peach-200/50 px-1 py-px font-mono text-foreground-800 text-sm dark:bg-mi=
dnight-900" style=3D"opacity: 1;">memtest86+</code> overnight to sniff out =
errors.</span></p></li></ul><div class=3D"relative pb-6 w-full after:border=
-b after:border-stroke-300 after:w-full after:absolute after:mt-3"></div><p=
><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">It=E2=80=99s wild how just one BIOS to=
ggle or boot config checkbox can kneecap a perfectly good setup. When you=
=E2=80=99re ready to tackle it, I can walk you through diagnostics or help =
write a health-check script to see what the system sees=E2=80=94or doesn=E2=
=80=99t.</span></p><p><span class=3D"font-ligatures-none">Want to zero in o=
n one of those areas first, or do a holistic sweep when you=E2=80=99ve got =
